  "hints": [
    "Each left edge changes column by -1 and each right edge by +1.",
    "A breadth-first queue processes nodes by row and left-to-right position, resolving ties without value sorting.",
    "Queue (root,0). Append each value to its column; enqueue its left child before its right child.",
    "Track the minimum and maximum column and emit those lists in increasing column order."
  ],
  "editorial": "Breadth-first traversal preserves the exact within-column order: shallower nodes precede deeper ones, and children are enqueued left to right. Store each visited value under its horizontal column. Columns between the minimum and maximum all exist because edges change the column by one. Empty input returns an empty list. The independent oracle computes binary paths, then sorts by column, path length, and path rather than using the queue reference.",
